From 8367da2720735d4a16e4af23f1626e72e925f639 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] client: Chrome's TLS 1.2 CBC and RSA key exchange suites, and
 its keyUsage checks

Chrome 157's ClientHello offers, after its AEAD ECDHE suites,
TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA, TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA,
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256, TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384,
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA and TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA. Epoxy sent
the same list (chrome_hello) but rustls implements none of them, so a
server that picked one failed with SelectedUnofferedCipherSuite:
pro.ip-api.com (geoguessr's geolocation), cs.pgammedia.com,
sync.pinklion.io and cs.playdigo.com pick AES256-SHA or AES128-SHA from
Chrome's offer.

The provider now has those six suites, in Chrome's order after its own.
The RSA ones use rustls's RSA key transport (rustls 0053) with
RSAES-PKCS1-v1_5 from RustCrypto's `rsa` (keys of 2048 to 8192 bits, the
sizes webpki takes for an ECDHE_RSA signature). The CBC records are TLS
1.2's MAC-then-encrypt with HMAC-SHA1 and an explicit random IV per record
(RustCrypto `aes`, constant time), opened in constant time as BoringSSL
opens them (tls_cbc.cc): the padding check, the HMAC over a secret length
(on the `sha1` crate's compression function) and the MAC's extraction take
the same time whatever the padding says. Nothing Chrome does not offer is
added: no 3DES, no other CBC or DHE suite, no SHA-256 MAC, no
encrypt_then_mac.

It also checks the end entity's keyUsage as BoringSSL does, which webpki
does not: a key that signs the handshake (an ECDHE suite, TLS 1.3) must
allow digitalSignature and one the pre-master secret is encrypted to must
allow keyEncipherment. Chrome refuses either mismatch with
ERR_SSL_KEY_USAGE_INCOMPATIBLE, and epoxy connected. The chain and name
verification is webpki's, unchanged, for every suite.
